/-!
# Index of the paper

This module restates, in the order of the paper, the theorems, propositions, lemmas,
corollaries, examples, remarks and figures of

> Yusuke Watanabe, *Counting the Fixed Points of Binary Belief Propagation:
> Generic Degree, Syzygies, and Real Solutions*,

followed by its supplementary material (SM2–SM5), and attaches the corresponding Lean
declarations.

* Every `theorem` and `alias` in this namespace is proved without `sorry` and is checked by
  `lake build`. The status describes its correspondence with the labelled paper statement,
  not whether its Lean proof is complete.
* **Proved**: the named statement or explicitly named clause is covered in the definitions of
  this project (see "Differences in hypotheses" in `FORMALIZATION.md`).
* **Partial**: the stated part is proved, but the full labelled paper statement has further
  clauses or identifications that are not formalized.
* **Not formalized**: no Lean statement exists. The reasons are recorded in `FORMALIZATION.md`,
  which also maps every label of the paper and of its supplement to Lean declarations.
* **Definition** and **Construction**: a definition with no claim attached. **Proved
  auxiliary**: a proved ingredient of the labelled statement. **Conditional**: proved under a
  hypothesis stated explicitly in the declaration.
* The public declarations of `StatementOnly/` are proved as well; the directory name does not
  indicate a missing proof.

The docstring of each item starts with the label used in the paper and the status.

Notation. `G` is a finite multigraph (`Multigraph V E`; `Loopless` means no self-loops),
n = |V|, m = |E|, ν = m − n + c (`nu`), τ (`tau`), ρ = m − n + τ (`rho`),
wt(S) = ∏_v φ(d_S(v)) (`wt phi S`), Q(G) (`Q`); the generic degree N(G) is expressed by
`HasGenericDegree G n`, and β_{p,q} by `betti p q` (= Σ_{|S| = q, |S| − v(S) = p} wt(S)).
The BP parameters are a_e = e^{2J_e} and b_i = e^{2h_i} (a real coupling J_e ≠ 0 means
a_e > 0 and a_e ≠ 1; a real field h means b > 0); admissibility is (`eq:admissibility`).
-/

namespace BPFixedPoints.Paper

open Multigraph BP Polynomial

set_option linter.unusedSectionVars false

variable {V E : Type*} [DecidableEq V] [DecidableEq E] [Fintype V] [Fintype E] (G : Multigraph V E)

/-! ## §1 Introduction -/

/-- **`thm:structure` (N = Q)** Proved. The generic admissible complex degree of a loopless
multigraph is Q(G): on the nonvanishing locus of a nonzero polynomial, the admissible BP
solutions are exactly Q(G) in number and all simple. -/
theorem thm_structure_N_eq_Q (hG : G.Loopless) : G.HasGenericDegree G.Q :=
  G.hasGenericDegree_Q hG

/-- **`thm:structure` (Betti numbers (`intro:betti`))** Proved, with the declarations named
here. This declaration states the numerical part: the state-sum table
β_{p,q} = Σ_{|S|=q, |S|−v(S)=p} wt(S) sums to N(G), the rows satisfy 0 ≤ p ≤ ρ and row ρ is
nonzero. The finite free resolution with state bases is `incResolutionDifferential`, minimal by
`incResolutionDifferential_minimal` and of length exactly ρ by
`projectiveDimension_incidenceRing`. The internal pieces of Tor and their dimensions β_{p,q}
are `incidenceTorInternalEquivState` and `finrank_incidenceTorInternalPiece`. -/
theorem thm_structure_betti (hG : G.Loopless) :
    G.HasGenericDegree (∑ p ∈ Finset.range (Fintype.card E + 1),
      ∑ q ∈ Finset.range (Fintype.card E + 1), G.betti p q) ∧
    (∀ p q, G.rho < p → G.betti p q = 0) ∧ ∃ q, G.betti G.rho q ≠ 0 :=
  ⟨G.hasGenericDegree_sum_betti hG, fun _ q hp => G.betti_eq_zero_of_rho_lt hp q,
    G.exists_betti_rho_ne_zero⟩

/-- **`thm:structure` (alternating numerator in (`intro:hilbert`))** Proved (polynomial identity).
The polynomial identity θ_G(t, 2i) = Σ_{p,q} (−1)^p β_{p,q} t^q is proved for the state-sum
table `betti`, identified with graded Tor dimensions by `finrank_incidenceTorInternalPiece`.
Its realization as the actual Hilbert numerator is `alg_hilbert_theta` below. -/
theorem thm_structure_theta :
    G.thetaGen (2 * Complex.I) = (G.thetaTwoI).map (Int.castRingHom ℂ) ∧
    G.thetaTwoI = ∑ p ∈ Finset.range (Fintype.card E + 1),
      ∑ q ∈ Finset.range (Fintype.card E + 1), C ((-1) ^ p * (G.betti p q : ℤ)) * X ^ q :=
  ⟨G.thetaGen_two_I, G.thetaTwoI_eq_betti⟩

/-- **`thm:structure` (ω̃_G = ω_G/(1−t)^τ = ∏_C ω_C)** Proved (polynomial identities).
(1−t)^n θ_G(t,2i) = (1−t)^m ω_G (that is, ω_G = (1−t)^{n−m} θ_G), ω_G = (1−t)^τ ω̃_G,
θ_G(t,2i) = (1−t)^ρ ω̃_G, ω̃_G is the product of ω_C over the components C that contain a
cycle, and θ_C = (1−t)^{ν(C)−1} ω_C for each such C. Its realization as the Hilbert series
of a regular linear Artin quotient is `alg_artin_hilbert`. -/
theorem thm_structure_omega :
    (1 - X : ℤ[X]) ^ Fintype.card V * G.thetaTwoI = (1 - X) ^ Fintype.card E * G.omega ∧
    G.omega = (1 - X) ^ G.tau * G.omegaTilde ∧
    G.thetaTwoI = (1 - X) ^ G.rho * G.omegaTilde ∧
    G.omegaTilde = ∏ K ∈ Finset.univ.filter (fun K => ¬ G.IsTreeComp K), G.compOmega K ∧
    ∀ K, (G.compGraph K).thetaTwoI = (1 - X) ^ ((G.compGraph K).cycleRank - 1) * G.compOmega K :=
  ⟨G.one_sub_pow_mul_thetaTwoI, rfl, G.thetaTwoI_eq_omegaTilde, rfl, G.thetaTwoI_compGraph⟩

/-- **`thm:structure` (dimension d_G = 3m + n − τ)** Proved (arithmetic of the symbols):
d_G + ρ = 4m. That the Krull dimension of 𝒰_G equals d_G is `ringKrullDim_incidenceRing`. -/
theorem thm_structure_dim : G.dG + G.rho = 4 * Fintype.card E := G.dG_add_rho

/-- **`thm:real` (i) (exhaustion of the complex fiber)** Proved. Fix a_e ≠ 0 with a_e² ≠ 1.
There is ε > 0 such that for 0 < |p_i| < ε (p_i = b_i⁻¹) the admissible complex solutions
are exactly Q(G) in number and all simple. The power-map charts (`intro:power-map`) are
`sf_normal_form`. The boundary local algebras are the local algebras of the analytic special
fibers of the state charts, of length wt(S) (`sf_state_fiber_equiv`, `sf_state_fiber_length`). -/
theorem thm_real_i (hG : G.Loopless) {a : E → ℂ} (ha : ∀ e, a e ≠ 0) (ha1 : ∀ e, a e ^ 2 ≠ 1) :
    ∃ ε > 0, ∀ p : V → ℂ, (∀ i, p i ≠ 0) → (∀ i, ‖p i‖ < ε) →
      Nat.card {y : Dart E → ℂ // G.IsAdmissibleSolution a (fun i => (p i)⁻¹) y} = G.Q ∧
      ∀ y, G.IsAdmissibleSolution a (fun i => (p i)⁻¹) y →
        G.IsSimpleSolution a (fun i => (p i)⁻¹) y :=
  strong_field_exactly_Q_simple hG ha ha1

/-- **`thm:real` (ii) (every Eulerian sector is nonempty)** Proved. At every field, every
Eulerian edge set is the negative-message support of some admissible real root. -/
theorem thm_real_ii_sector (hG : G.Loopless) {a : E → ℝ} (ha : ∀ e, 0 < a e) (ha1 : ∀ e, a e ≠ 1)
    {b : V → ℝ} (hb : ∀ i, 0 < b i) {S : Finset E} (hS : G.IsEvenSet S) :
    ∃ y, G.IsAdmissibleSolution a b y ∧ negSupport y = S :=
  exists_real_solution ha ha1 hG hS hb

/-- **`thm:real` (ii) (lower bound R ≥ 2^ν)** Proved. At every field there are at least 2^ν
admissible real roots (`ENat.card`, so finiteness is not assumed). -/
theorem thm_real_ii_lower (hG : G.Loopless) {a : E → ℝ} (ha : ∀ e, 0 < a e) (ha1 : ∀ e, a e ≠ 1)
    {b : V → ℝ} (hb : ∀ i, 0 < b i) :
    ((2 ^ G.nu : ℕ) : ℕ∞) ≤ ENat.card {y : Dart E → ℝ // G.IsAdmissibleSolution a b y} := by
  rw [← card_evenSets_eq_two_pow_nu]
  exact card_evenSets_le_real ha ha1 hG hb

/-- **`thm:real` (ii) (the three counts at strong field (`intro:three-counts`))** Proved.
If |h_i| is large enough at every vertex (with arbitrary signs of the field), then
(complex, real, positive) = (Q(G), 2^ν, 1) and all complex roots are simple. -/
theorem thm_real_ii_strong (hG : G.Loopless) {a : E → ℝ} (ha : ∀ e, 0 < a e)
    (ha1 : ∀ e, a e ≠ 1) :
    ∃ H : ℝ, ∀ b : V → ℝ, (∀ i, 0 < b i) → (∀ i, H ≤ |Real.log (b i)|) →
      Nat.card {y : Dart E → ℂ //
          G.IsAdmissibleSolution (fun e => (a e : ℂ)) (fun i => (b i : ℂ)) y} = G.Q ∧
      (∀ y, G.IsAdmissibleSolution (fun e => (a e : ℂ)) (fun i => (b i : ℂ)) y →
        G.IsSimpleSolution (fun e => (a e : ℂ)) (fun i => (b i : ℂ)) y) ∧
      Nat.card {y : Dart E → ℝ // G.IsAdmissibleSolution a b y} = 2 ^ G.nu ∧
      Nat.card {y : Dart E → ℝ // G.IsAdmissibleSolution a b y ∧ ∀ α, 0 < y α} = 1 := by
  rw [← card_evenSets_eq_two_pow_nu]
  exact G.strong_field_three_counts hG ha ha1

/-- **`thm:real` (ii) (minimum real count (`intro:minimum`), (`real:minimum`))** Proved.
min_h R_G(J, h) = 2^ν: at least 2^ν at every field, and exactly 2^ν (finitely many) at some
field. -/
theorem thm_real_ii_min (hG : G.Loopless) {a : E → ℝ} (ha : ∀ e, 0 < a e) (ha1 : ∀ e, a e ≠ 1) :
    (∀ b : V → ℝ, (∀ i, 0 < b i) →
        ((2 ^ G.nu : ℕ) : ℕ∞) ≤ ENat.card {y : Dart E → ℝ // G.IsAdmissibleSolution a b y}) ∧
      ∃ b : V → ℝ, (∀ i, 0 < b i) ∧
        Finite {y : Dart E → ℝ // G.IsAdmissibleSolution a b y} ∧
        Nat.card {y : Dart E → ℝ // G.IsAdmissibleSolution a b y} = 2 ^ G.nu ∧
        Nat.card {y : Dart E → ℝ // G.IsAdmissibleSolution a b y ∧ ∀ α, 0 < y α} = 1 := by
  rw [← card_evenSets_eq_two_pow_nu]
  exact min_real_count ha ha1 hG

/-- **(`intro:generator-real-bridge`)** Proved in state-sum form for maximum degree at most 3.
The intrinsic module generator count is `alg_module_generator_number`, and its equality with
the minimum real count is `alg_module_generator_min_real_count`. -/
theorem intro_generator_real_bridge (hG : G.Loopless) (hdeg : ∀ v, G.deg Finset.univ v ≤ 3)
    {a : E → ℝ} (ha : ∀ e, 0 < a e) (ha1 : ∀ e, a e ≠ 1) :
    ∑ q ∈ Finset.range (Fintype.card E + 1), G.betti 0 q = 2 ^ G.nu ∧
      (∀ b : V → ℝ, (∀ i, 0 < b i) →
        ((2 ^ G.nu : ℕ) : ℕ∞) ≤ ENat.card {y : Dart E → ℝ // G.IsAdmissibleSolution a b y}) ∧
      ∃ b : V → ℝ, (∀ i, 0 < b i) ∧
        Nat.card {y : Dart E → ℝ // G.IsAdmissibleSolution a b y} = 2 ^ G.nu := by
  obtain ⟨h1, b, hb, -, h2, -⟩ := thm_real_ii_min G hG ha ha1
  exact ⟨G.sum_betti_zero_eq_of_deg_le_three hdeg, h1, b, hb, h2⟩

/-! ## §2 The admissible BP family and the generic degree -/

/-- **`ex:triangle-admissible`** Proved. The triangle with a_e = b_i = 2: the rational BP
equations have 4 real solutions, of which 2 are admissible, = N(C₃); the mixed solutions have
F_i = −1. -/
theorem ex_triangle_admissible :
    Nat.card {y : Dart (Fin 3) → ℝ //
      Examples.triangle.IsRationalFixedPoint (fun _ => (2 : ℝ)) (fun _ => (2 : ℝ)) y} = 4 ∧
    Nat.card {y : Dart (Fin 3) → ℝ //
      Examples.triangle.IsAdmissibleSolution (fun _ => (2 : ℝ)) (fun _ => (2 : ℝ)) y} = 2 ∧
    Examples.triangle.HasGenericDegree 2 ∧
    ∀ i, Examples.triangle.totalField (fun _ => (2 : ℝ))
      (Examples.triSol (Examples.triRoot true) (Examples.triRoot false)) i = -1 :=
  ⟨Examples.card_triangle_rational, Examples.card_triangle_admissible, Examples.triangle_N,
    fun i => Examples.triangle_totalField_mixed (Examples.triRoot_spec true)
      (Examples.triRoot_spec false) (fun h => by
        have := Examples.triRoot_injective h; simp at this) i⟩

/-- **`ex:triangle-admissible` (the map T³)** Proved. T³(x) − x = −5(2x² − 2x − 1)/(10x + 4)
(the computation of Example `ex:triangle-admissible`). -/
alias ex_triangle_T_cube := Examples.triT_iter_three_sub

/-- **`prop:bp-family` (counting form of generic finite étaleness)** Proved. Over a nonempty
Zariski open set (the nonvanishing locus of a nonzero polynomial) the fiber consists of exactly
N(G) = Q(G) points, all simple. Smoothness is `prop_bp_family_smooth`; the domain property and
the dimension are `familyRing_isDomain` and `ringKrullDim_familyRing`. -/
theorem prop_bp_family (hG : G.Loopless) : G.HasGenericDegree G.Q := G.hasGenericDegree_Q hG

/-- **`prop:bp-family` (smoothness)** Proved. The coordinate ring of the admissible BP total
space is smooth over ℂ. -/
theorem prop_bp_family_smooth {V E : Type} [DecidableEq V] [Fintype V] [DecidableEq E]
    [Fintype E] (G : Multigraph V E) (hG : G.Loopless) : Algebra.Smooth ℂ G.FamilyRing :=
  G.familyRing_smooth hG

/-- **`lem:weak-boundary`** Proved (valuation form). For any valuation v on a field with
v(s_e − 1) < 1, v(ζ_i) = 1 and v(ζ_i + 1) = 1, every admissible auxiliary solution satisfies
v(F_i) = v(A_{e,i}) = v(1 + F_i) = 1. The application to the paper's 𝒦 = \overline{K((t))}
is `boundary_exclusion_puiseux`. -/
alias lem_weak_boundary := Multigraph.boundary_exclusion

/-- **`lem:unique-weak-lift` (Hensel lifting)** Proved. Every limit point lifts uniquely to
a solution over K⟦t⟧. -/
alias lem_unique_weak_lift := Multigraph.exists_unique_lift

/-- **`lem:unique-weak-lift` (uniqueness over a valuation ring)** Proved. Two solutions with
the same residue whose Jacobian determinant is a unit coincide (completeness is not used). -/
alias lem_unique_weak_lift_uniqueness := BP.unique_of_cong

/-- **`lem:unique-weak-lift` (Q distinct admissible formal solutions)** Proved. -/
alias lem_unique_weak_lift_count := Multigraph.exists_Q_formal_solutions

/-- **`thm:degree`** Proved. N(G) = Q(G) (through the definition `HasGenericDegree` and its
uniqueness `HasGenericDegree.unique`). -/
theorem thm_degree (hG : G.Loopless) {n : ℕ} : G.HasGenericDegree n ↔ n = G.Q :=
  G.hasGenericDegree_iff hG

/-- **`cor:elementary-degree` (disjoint union)** Proved. -/
alias cor_elementary_degree_disjUnion := Multigraph.hasGenericDegree_disjUnion

/-- **`cor:elementary-degree` (attaching a leaf)** Proved. -/
alias cor_elementary_degree_addLeaf := Multigraph.hasGenericDegree_addLeaf_iff

/-- **`cor:elementary-degree` (subdividing an edge)** Proved. -/
alias cor_elementary_degree_subdivide := Multigraph.hasGenericDegree_subdivide_iff

/-- **`cor:elementary-degree` (forests)** Proved. If ν = 0 (the only Eulerian edge set is the
empty set), then N = 1. -/
theorem cor_elementary_degree_forest (hG : G.Loopless) (hν : G.nu = 0) : G.HasGenericDegree 1 := by
  refine G.hasGenericDegree_one_of_forest hG fun S hS => ?_
  have h := G.card_evenSets_eq_two_pow_nu
  rw [hν, pow_zero, Finset.card_eq_one] at h
  obtain ⟨T, hT⟩ := h
  have h1 : S ∈ Finset.univ.filter G.IsEvenSet := by simp [hS]
  have h2 : (∅ : Finset E) ∈ Finset.univ.filter G.IsEvenSet := by simp [IsEvenSet]
  rw [hT, Finset.mem_singleton] at h1 h2
  rw [h1, h2]

/-- **`cor:elementary-degree` (a single cycle)** Proved. If G is connected with ν = 1, then
N = 2. -/
theorem cor_elementary_degree_unicyclic (hG : G.Loopless) (hc : G.Connected) (hν : G.nu = 1) :
    G.HasGenericDegree 2 := by
  rw [G.nu_eq_cycleRank_of_connected hc] at hν
  rw [G.hasGenericDegree_iff hG, G.Q_eq_two_of_cycleRank_eq_one hc hν]

/-! ## §3 The universal edge-incidence ring -/

/-- **`alg:state-cohomology`** Proved, with `alg_state_dimensions`. The combinatorial expressions
Σ_{|S|=q, v(S)=j} wt(S) (`stateDim`) are proved to sum to Q; the nonzero entries satisfy
0 ≤ q − j ≤ ρ, and the upper bound is attained by a contributing state. The sheaf cohomology
of each summand L(−d_S), its concentration in degree v(S) and its rank wt(S) are
`alg_vertex_twist_cohomology_states` and `alg_vertex_twist_cohomology_finrank`. The dimension
formula for E_1 is `alg_state_dimensions`. -/
theorem alg_state_cohomology :
    (∑ q ∈ Finset.range (Fintype.card E + 1), ∑ j ∈ Finset.range (Fintype.card V + 1),
      G.stateDim q j = G.Q) ∧
    (∀ q j, (q < j ∨ G.rho < q - j) → G.stateDim q j = 0) ∧
    ∃ S : Finset E, G.IsContributing S ∧ S.card = (G.support S).card + G.rho :=
  ⟨G.sum_stateDim_eq_Q, fun _ _ h => G.stateDim_eq_zero_of h, G.exists_isContributing_card_eq⟩

/-- **`alg:universal-definition`** Definition only (`IncidenceRing`, the intersection of
the affine chart images in the torus ring). Its natural coefficient grading is
`incidenceCoefficientGradedAlgebra`. Its actual projective-zero global-section
comparison is `alg_projective_zero_global_sections` below. -/
noncomputable abbrev alg_universal_definition {V E : Type} [DecidableEq V] [Fintype V] [DecidableEq E]
    [Fintype E] (G : Multigraph V E) := G.IncidenceRing

/- **`alg:incidence-vector-bundle`** Partial. Proved: finiteness over S_G, `incidenceRing_finite`
(`StatementOnly/IncidenceRing.lean`); (𝒰_G)_0 = ℂ (`alg_degree_zero`); the closed embedding
into the product of projective lines (`alg_incidence_projective_closed`), the comparison with
the independent homogeneous zero scheme and its charts (`alg_incidence_projective_zero_scheme`,
`alg_incidence_projective_zero_charts`) and its global functions
(`alg_projective_zero_global_sections`); properness, also after every base change
(`alg_incidence_projective_proper`, `alg_incidence_proper_basechange`). The vector-bundle
presentation is formalized on charts only (`alg_vector_bundle_regular`,
`alg_vector_bundle_trivialization`, `alg_vector_bundle_evaluation_surjective`,
`alg_vector_bundle_kernel_rank`); the global kernel sheaf, the gluing of the trivializations
and the relative Spec are not formalized. -/

/- **`alg:resolution`** Partial. Proved: the numerical Betti table (`betti`), the row range
and the nonvanishing of row ρ (`thm_structure_betti`). Two finite contractions of the
Koszul–Čech complex give a finite free resolution of the incidence ring, minimal at
the coefficient origin, and the projective dimension ρ (`projectiveDimension_incidenceRing`).
The homogeneous free terms specialize to internal pieces of actual Tor of dimension β_{p,q}
(`finrank_incidenceTorInternalPiece`), with finite unique decomposition. The natural grading
on the incidence ring, its augmentation and exactness in each internal degree are proved in
`NaturalGradedResolution.lean`. The edge exterior product and full Čech cup product satisfy
Leibniz and are compatible with the augmentation. Their transferred product is the standard
product on actual derived Tor (`alg_tor_product`), and the bigraded Tor algebra is isomorphic
to the cohomology of the exterior bundle (`alg_tor_isomorphism`, `alg_tor_isomorphism_mul`).
The free terms are identified with sheaf cohomology only through state bases
(`alg_exterior_bundle_states`); the concentration of the derived global sections of the
Koszul complex of sheaves is not formalized. -/

/-- **`alg:tor-isomorphism` (product comparison)** Proved for the concrete Koszul–Čech
model: the standard product on actual derived Tor agrees with the projected exterior-edge
and Čech cup product at the coefficient origin. Independence of the multiplication lift is
`incidenceTorProduct_eq_of_lift`. The comparison with sheaf cohomology is
`alg_tor_isomorphism_mul`. -/
alias alg_tor_product := Multigraph.incidenceTorProduct_cech_formula

/-- **`alg:tor-isomorphism` (associativity)** Proved for the actual derived Tor product. -/
alias alg_tor_associative := Multigraph.incidenceTorProduct_assoc

/-- **`alg:tor-isomorphism` (graded commutativity)** Proved with sign (-1)^(pq). -/
alias alg_tor_graded_comm := Multigraph.incidenceTorProduct_graded_comm

/-- **`alg:tor-isomorphism` (left unit)** Proved using a lift of the unit of the incidence ring. -/
alias alg_tor_unit_left := Multigraph.incidenceTorOne_mul

/-- **`alg:tor-isomorphism` (right unit)** Proved for the same unit. -/
alias alg_tor_unit_right := Multigraph.incidenceTor_mul_one

/-- **`alg:tor-isomorphism` (internal grading)** Proved. The standard product adds the natural
internal degrees as well as the homological degrees. -/
alias alg_tor_product_internal_degree := Multigraph.incidenceTorProduct_internal_degree

/-- **`alg:tor-isomorphism` (unit degree)** Proved. The unit has bidegree (0,0). -/
alias alg_tor_unit_internal_degree := Multigraph.incidenceTorOne_internal_degree

/-- **`alg:total-betti`** Proved, with `sum_finrank_incidenceTor`. This declaration proves
N(G) = Q(G) = Σ_{p,q} β_{p,q} for the numerical state-sum table `betti`. The total dimension of
derived specialization is Q(G) by `sum_finrank_incidenceTor`, and the naturally graded Tor
pieces have dimensions β_{p,q} by `finrank_incidenceTorInternalPiece`. -/
theorem alg_total_betti (hG : G.Loopless) :
    G.HasGenericDegree G.Q ∧
      ∑ p ∈ Finset.range (Fintype.card E + 1), ∑ q ∈ Finset.range (Fintype.card E + 1),
        G.betti p q = G.Q :=
  ⟨G.hasGenericDegree_Q hG, G.sum_betti_eq_Q⟩

/-- **`alg:normal-cm` (normal domain)** Proved, from the polynomial presentation of the charts,
the injection into the torus and integral closedness. The Krull dimension is
`ringKrullDim_incidenceRing`. The Cohen–Macaulay clause is `alg_cohenMacaulay` below. -/
theorem alg_normal_domain {V E : Type} [DecidableEq V] [Fintype V] [DecidableEq E]
    [Fintype E] (G : Multigraph V E) :
    IsDomain G.IncidenceRing ∧ IsIntegrallyClosed G.IncidenceRing :=
  ⟨G.incidenceRing_isDomain, G.incidenceRing_isIntegrallyClosed⟩

/-- **`alg:normal-cm` (Cohen–Macaulay)** Proved for the chart-intersection ring:
it is Noetherian and at every maximal ideal its localization has depth equal to Krull
dimension. Both values are d_G (`incidence_local_depth`, `incidence_local_krullDim`). -/
alias alg_cohenMacaulay := Multigraph.incidence_cohenMacaulay

/-- **`alg:normal-cm` (coefficient-origin depth)** Proved. The depth of the actual
incidence module at the coefficient-origin ideal is d_G. -/
alias alg_origin_depth := Multigraph.incidenceDepth_eq

/-- **`alg:hilbert` (existence of regular Artin quotients)** Proved. In every maximal
ideal there is a regular sequence of length d_G whose actual quotient is nonzero,
Artinian and finite-dimensional over ℂ. Linear cuts at the origin are treated below. -/
alias alg_regular_artin_exists := Multigraph.exists_incidence_regular_artin_quotient

/-- **`alg:hilbert` (linear Artin quotient)** Proved. There is a length-d_G regular
sequence of homogeneous linear coefficient forms. Its actual quotient is nonzero,
Artinian, finite-dimensional, with Hilbert series omega-tilde and dimension omega-tilde(1).
The nonempty Zariski-open finite-quotient locus is treated by `alg_artin_parameter_open`. -/
alias alg_linear_artin_exists := Multigraph.exists_incidence_linear_artin_hilbert

/-- **`alg:artin-hilbert`** Proved for every homogeneous linear regular sequence of
length d_G in the chart-intersection ring, with the grading induced by that ring. -/
alias alg_artin_hilbert := Multigraph.incidenceArtinHilbertSeries_eq

/-- **`alg:artin-hilbert` (length)** Proved: the complex dimension of the actual
Artin quotient is omega-tilde(1). -/
alias alg_artin_length := Multigraph.finrank_incidence_linear_artin

/-- **`alg:hilbert` (parameter openness)** Proved. All length-d_G coefficient tuples
whose actual quotient is finite-dimensional form a nonempty Zariski-open set.
Every quotient there is nonzero and Artinian. The regularity and common Hilbert
series on the whole locus are proved in `alg_artin_parameters_regular` below. -/
alias alg_artin_parameter_open := Multigraph.incidence_finite_parameter_open

/-- **`alg:hilbert` (principal open certificate)** Proved. A polynomial nonvanishing
condition with an actual witness guarantees nonzero finite-dimensional Artin quotients. -/
alias alg_artin_parameter_certificate := Multigraph.exists_incidence_parameter_certificate

/-- **`alg:hilbert` (the full open parameter locus)** Proved. Every tuple on the
nonempty open finite-quotient locus is regular in its specified order; its actual
quotient has the induced Hilbert series omega-tilde and dimension omega-tilde(1). -/
alias alg_artin_parameters_regular := Multigraph.incidence_parameter_regular_open

/-- **`alg:hilbert` (finite normalization by linear parameters)** Proved. Every
finite-quotient tuple gives a finite injective map from the polynomial ring on d_G
variables into the original ring, and its parameter elements are algebraically independent. -/
alias alg_parameter_normalization := Multigraph.incidence_parameter_normalization

/-- **`alg:hilbert` (homogeneous freeness over the parameters)** Proved. At every finite-quotient
parameter tuple the original ring is finite free and faithfully flat over its polynomial
parameter algebra, with rank omega-tilde(1). The proof constructs a homogeneous basis. -/
alias alg_parameter_finite_free := Multigraph.incidence_parameter_finite_free

/-- **`alg:hilbert` (arbitrary base change)** Proved. Every nonzero base change of this
parameter algebra preserves finite freeness, faithful flatness and rank omega-tilde(1). -/
alias alg_parameter_baseChange := Multigraph.incidence_parameter_baseChange

/-- **`alg:hilbert` (actual parameter quotients)** Proved. The original quotient by the
evaluated relations is algebra-isomorphic to the tensor base change. Every proper parameter
ideal therefore gives a finite free, faithfully flat quotient of the same rank. -/
alias alg_parameter_cut := Multigraph.incidence_parameter_cut_finite_free

/-- **`alg:pencil-sections` (Hilbert series of partial sections)** Proved. Cutting the first
r of any finite-quotient parameter tuple leaves Hilbert series omega-tilde/(1-t)^(d_G-r),
for 0 <= r <= d_G, with the grading induced by the original ring. -/
alias alg_parameter_section_hilbert := Multigraph.incidenceParameterSection_hilbert

/-- **`alg:pencil-sections` (pencil section)** Proved. For d_G > 0 the first
d_G-1 cuts have Hilbert series omega-tilde/(1-t). The resolution after the cuts is
`alg_parameter_resolution_exact`. -/
alias alg_pencil_hilbert := Multigraph.incidenceParameterPencil_hilbert

/-- **`alg:pencil-sections` (coefficient resolution)** Proved. For every finite-quotient
parameter tuple and every parameter base change, the base change of the coefficient
differentials is exact. `incidenceParameterBaseChangeResolution` includes the augmentation. -/
alias alg_parameter_resolution_exact := Multigraph.incidenceParameterBaseChange_exact

/-- **`alg:pencil-sections` (minimality)** Proved. Every compatible residue map of the changed
coefficient ring preserves minimality of the scalar-extended original resolution. -/
alias alg_parameter_resolution_minimal := Multigraph.incidenceParameterBaseChangeResolution_minimal

/-- **`alg:pencil-sections` (Tor under parameter relations)** Proved. Any parameter ideal
through the origin preserves the actual Tor dimensions in each homological degree. -/
alias alg_parameter_cut_tor := Multigraph.finrank_incidenceParameterCutTor

/-- **`alg:pencil-sections` (successive linear cuts)** Proved. The first r linear cuts,
including the pencil and Artin cuts, preserve each homological row of actual Tor dimensions.
The natural internal components are compared in `alg_parameter_section_bigraded`. -/
alias alg_parameter_section_tor := Multigraph.finrank_incidenceSectionTor

/-- **`alg:pencil-sections` (total Betti number)** Proved. Every successive section has
total actual Tor dimension Q(G), over its correspondingly cut coefficient ring. -/
alias alg_parameter_section_total_betti := Multigraph.sum_finrank_incidenceSectionTor

/-- **`alg:pencil-sections` (bigraded Betti table)** Proved. The natural internal components
of actual Tor after every successive linear cut have dimensions beta_{p,q}. -/
alias alg_parameter_section_bigraded := Multigraph.finrank_incidenceSectionTorInternalPiece

/-- **`alg:pencil-sections` (graded resolution)** Proved. Homogeneous parameter cuts through
a finite-quotient tuple preserve exactness in every internal degree. -/
alias alg_parameter_resolution_graded_exact := Multigraph.incidenceCutDifferential_graded_exact

/-- **`alg:pencil-sections` (graded augmentation)** Proved. The kernel of the scalar-extended
augmentation consists of boundaries of the same natural internal degree. -/
alias alg_parameter_augmentation_graded_exact := Multigraph.incidenceCutAugmentation_graded_exact

/-- **`alg:pencil-sections` (multiplication)** Proved. Actual Tor after every parameter
cut has the product obtained by scalar extension of an original multiplication lift;
comparison to original Tor preserves it. -/
alias alg_parameter_cut_tor_product := Multigraph.incidenceParameterCutTorEquiv_mul

/-- **`alg:pencil-sections` (quotient naturality)** Proved. On every free representative,
